{
  "title": "Calibrate Polygraphia row schedules and unknown-key recovery on independent tables",
  "program": "L3",
  "on_path": true,
  "hard": false,
  "statement": "For a follow-up fixed locally before reading public evaluation results, use the prior pilot\u2019s 40 and 48 initial rows, stay probability 1/8, 5,000 plaintext letters at offsets 20,000\u201324,999 of Augustine, Erasmus and Nibelungenlied, and schedule seeds 2001\u20132003. Every clean sample is inverted exactly using its known row schedule and table. Add a separate 2% independent raw-character substitution noise condition, preserving word length. Four exploratory acceptance intervals centered on the prior public-training values are word entropy 8.795\u20139.795 bits, raw glyph entropy 3.636\u20134.036, within-word conditional glyph entropy 1.336\u20131.736, and adjacent edit-one share 0.0291\u20130.0591. On 18 clean and 18 noisy samples, zero meet all four fixed intervals under either public evaluation layout; all miss the conditional-glyph range. On the ZL layout clean conditional entropy spans 2.312\u20132.503 bits and noisy 2.117\u20132.286, versus public ZL 1.517 and IT 1.557. Those intervals are hand-chosen sensitivities around an already inspected training pilot, not calibrated acceptance thresholds. Public evaluation was measured after locally freezing the plan; the earlier row counts were selected adaptively, no independent timestamped preregistration was made, and the actual historical table is shared. This is a descriptive constraint on this narrow procedure, not a cell exclusion or unknown-key recovery.",
  "check": "Fix a broader historical schedule family and noise model before examining independent works or layouts; use separately drawn cipher tables or distinct historical tables when available. Calibrate uncertainty and joint acceptance via independent positive and null controls, examine source and tokenization sensitivity, and charge the full row schedule and lexicon when used as a reading. Implement a reviewed declarative evaluator and unknown-key recovery with blinded, disjoint planted keys before using any cipher-family verdict.",
  "method": {
    "input": "Fixed historical table from https://github.com/hermesj/R_Voynich_Stats/blob/0ef1439fa9d90aeab42951a51d0d5a0e27db1f8f/R%20code/Historical_ciphers/keys/ciphers_poly_III_in_order.csv SHA-256 fe3f24a46dbc6b655eab8dbbd75addf84ee4a7dbd387ad802af75d02edfe5b4d. Plaintexts are pinned Gutenberg files normalized by refcorpus.blocks_of. Their selected windows have not been editorially audited and may contain edition or transcription material.",
    "layouts": "First 5,000 certain lowercase ASCII paragraph tokens on each ZL/IT public practice-evaluation split; adjacency never bridges uncertain tokens or line ends. Four metrics and tokenization follow the P-L3-009 embedded helper.",
    "selection": "Prewritten local plan tests 40,48 rows and three fresh schedule seeds at 1/8 stay, each in three different plaintext windows. These row sizes stem from an earlier adaptive search. Acceptance intervals are broad round margins chosen from that earlier training sample, not historical confidence bounds.",
    "noise": "Each cipher word\u2019s raw character independently changes with probability .02 to another character sampled uniformly from the clean sample glyph inventory; fixed seed by schedule and row count. This can produce a word absent from the fixed table, and is a synthetic transcription perturbation, not a historical noise model. Clean inversion discloses the row schedule; no claim of unknown-key decoding.",
    "limits": "Only three related runs per book and one fixed table; manuscript witnesses are alternate transcriptions of the same object, not independent manuscripts. Acceptance intervals are exploratory and no production model or metadata lengths are paid."
  },
